    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 192202\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n302 PM PDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming trough brings widespread rain and mountain snow \n  chances, gusty winds, and below normal temperatures midweek. \n  \n* Increasing confidence for a dry conditions and warming \n  temperatures this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the Weekend.\n\nAn upper level trough, currently centered off the coast of Oregon, \nwill bring significant chances in the weather pattern this week as \nit plunges south and east. Precipitation chances start to appear \nover high terrain on Tuesday afternoon due to increasing moisture \nand difluence aloft. However, more widespread precipitation is \nexpected on Wednesday and Thursday as the trough moves into Southern \nCalifornia. Moisture advection brings PWATS into the 0.5 to 1.0 inch \nrange, which is 200 to 300 percent of normal for late October. \nElevated moisture combined with instability driven by strong winds \naloft and cold air under the low may prompt particularly efficient \nrain rates on Wednesday and Thursday. Forecast soundings show 500-\n1000 J/kg MUCAPE and 40+ knots of shear starting Wednesday afternoon \nin southern Nevada and northwestern Arizona. Snow levels remain \nabove 10,000 feet on Wednesday and fall to around 8,500 feet on \nThursday. Although the trough moves east by Friday, lingering \nmoisture and instability may support additional showers over high \nterrain. Forecast confidence in timing for specific locations should \nincrease with additional high resolution guidance. \n\nAnother notable impact will be increased winds starting Tuesday. 850 \nmb wind speeds increase to 25 to 30 knots. For Tuesday, the \nstrongest surface winds will be in the Owens Valley and southern \nGreat Basin. Peak gusts will generally be in the 25 to 35 mph range, \nwith a low probability (10-20%) of wind gusts reaching 40 mph in the \nafternoon. Southerly winds spread to most of the remainder of the \nforecast area on Wednesday as the low tracks southeast. Lingering \nsouthwesterly breezes remain across the Mojave Desert on Thursday \nbefore the system moves out. \n\nA ridge should develop this weekend as the trough moves off into the \nFour Corners. Moisture will scour out and dry conditions are \nexpected. Temperatures remain several degrees below normal for this \ntime of year, but will increase a degree or two compared to \ntemperatures midweek. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...\nFairly quiet and benign conditions expected for the next 24-30 \nhours. Light winds favoring an E to NE direction under 8KT continue \ninto the evening. Around 04z, light winds shift to a more WSW \ndirection and remain light and variable throughout the night. \nSimilar pattern expected Tuesday  with light E-NE winds developing \nafter 17-18z, becoming SE during the afternoon around and under 8 \nkt. VFR conditions will continue with a few passing clouds around 10-\n12kft. Isolated convection possible in higher terrain tomorrow but \nno impacts to KLAS are currently expected. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Quiet and benign \nconditions to persist through the TAF period for most sites. Winds \nfollow typical daily directional patterns and remain around 8KT or \nless the next 24 hours. The only exception will be KBIH, where \nsoutheasterly up-valley winds gusting to 15-25KT continue this \nevening, then redevelop again after 18z tomorrow gusting up to 30 \nkt. Elevated southerly winds may persist a few hours after sunset at \nKBIH before becoming light and northerly after 06z. VFR conditions \nprevail with no operationally significant cloud cover. Isolated \nconvection possible in higher terrain tomorrow but no impacts to \nterminals are currently expected.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
